From: mediaforge
Subject: [PATCH] lavc/codec_desc: mark AVS2 as AV_CODEC_PROP_REORDER

The AVS2 codec descriptor carries only AV_CODEC_PROP_LOSSY, while every
other modern inter-frame codec that supports B-frames (H.264, HEVC, VVC,
EVC, AV1, ...) also sets AV_CODEC_PROP_REORDER. libxavs2 emits a real
B-frame-reordered stream (default bf=7) with a correct decode-order DTS,
but ff_encode_encode_cb() in libavcodec/encode.c clobbers it with
dts = pts whenever the descriptor lacks REORDER:

    if (!(avctx->codec_descriptor->props & AV_CODEC_PROP_REORDER) ||
        !(avctx->codec->capabilities & AV_CODEC_CAP_DELAY)        ||
        (codec->caps_internal & FF_CODEC_CAP_EOF_FLUSH))
        avpkt->dts = avpkt->pts;

libxavs2 has AV_CODEC_CAP_DELAY and does not set FF_CODEC_CAP_EOF_FLUSH,
so the missing REORDER prop is the only reason its DTS is destroyed,
yielding non-monotonic DTS in coding order and a muxer EINVAL
("non monotonically increasing dts"). Adding REORDER preserves the
wrapper-provided DTS (encode.c) and makes the muxer treat the stream as
reordered (libavformat/mux.c sets sti->reorder=1). The AVS2 sequence
header already signals the reorder depth via output_reorder_delay, so a
conformant decoder reorders correctly. No decode/demux path reads this
prop, so decoding is unaffected. Brings AVS2 in line with VVC/EVC.
--- a/libavcodec/codec_desc.c
+++ b/libavcodec/codec_desc.c
@@ -1413,7 +1413,7 @@
         .type      = AVMEDIA_TYPE_VIDEO,
         .name      = "avs2",
         .long_name = NULL_IF_CONFIG_SMALL("AVS2-P2/IEEE1857.4"),
-        .props     = AV_CODEC_PROP_LOSSY,
+        .props     = AV_CODEC_PROP_LOSSY | AV_CODEC_PROP_REORDER,
     },
     {
         .id        = AV_CODEC_ID_PGX,
